"03_commctrl.mst — what the COMMON CONTROLS wave needs that no file provides.

 Two gaps, both invisible at load and both fatal at construction, which is
 why they are here rather than filed as future work.

 Loaded from `st/mvp_compat`, after `st/mvp`, because both reopen classes the
 translated wave defines."

"── CommCtrlLibrary: the init-flag ledger ──────────────────────────────
 Dolphin's control classes each declare, in their class-side `initialize`,
 which comctl32 class family they need:

     TreeView class >> initialize  ^CommCtrlLibrary addInitFlag: ICC_TREEVIEW_CLASSES

 and Dolphin's `CommCtrlLibrary class >> open` ORs the accumulated flags into
 one `INITCOMMONCONTROLSEX` before the first control is created. The protocol
 was never generated — `genprims.py` emits the external calls, and these are
 plain Smalltalk — so every one of those `initialize` methods raised
 `CommCtrlLibrary class has no method 'addInitFlag:'`. The boot helper
 contains such errors, so the visible symptom was four class variables
 silently left nil.

 STAND-IN, and a narrow one: this port does NOT call InitCommonControlsEx
 from here. The door does it, in `EnsureCommonControls()` on the
 class-registration path, with a fixed superset (ICC_WIN95_CLASSES and
 friends) — because the call must happen on the UI thread before the first
 control is created, and the door is the only code that knows when that is.
 So the ledger below RECORDS what Dolphin asked for and `open`/`openDefault`
 answer that the library is already up.

 RETIREMENT: when the door learns to ask Smalltalk for its flags rather than
 hard-coding a superset, `open` here becomes the real call and `initFlags`
 its argument. Until then the divergence is: a control family Dolphin did NOT
 ask for is initialized anyway. That is wasteful, never wrong — and it is
 recorded in docs/LOOSE_ENDS.md rather than left to be rediscovered."
CommCtrlLibrary extend [
    <classVars: InitFlags>

    CommCtrlLibrary class >> initFlags [ ^InitFlags ifNil: [ 0 ] ]
    CommCtrlLibrary class >> initFlags: anInteger [ InitFlags := anInteger ]

    CommCtrlLibrary class >> addInitFlag: anInteger [
        "Record that a control family is required. Answers the accumulated set."
        InitFlags := self initFlags bitOr: anInteger.
        ^InitFlags
    ]

    CommCtrlLibrary class >> removeInitFlag: anInteger [
        "The mirror, sent from `uninitialize` when a control class is unloaded."
        InitFlags := self initFlags bitAnd: anInteger bitInvert.
        ^InitFlags
    ]

    "`ControlView>>basicCreateWindow:` sends this before CreateWindowExW.
     True because the door already ran InitCommonControlsEx; see above."
    CommCtrlLibrary class >> open [ ^true ]
    CommCtrlLibrary class >> openDefault [ ^self open ]
    CommCtrlLibrary class >> isOpen [ ^true ]
]

"NOTHING ELSE BELONGS HERE. `TreeView`'s `UpdateModes`, `ListView`'s
 `LvModes` and `IconicListAbstract`'s `ViewModes` were all nil on the
 construction path too, and each was briefly a hand-written table in this
 file. They are not, now: all three are `classConstants:` entries whose values
 ARE in the corpus, as multi-line expressions that the translator's entry
 regex truncated. `pools.class_constant_entries` reads them properly and
 `emit_class` emits a `initializeClassConstants` per class, called at the
 class's own place in the load order — which fixed nine classes, not three.
 See docs/JOURNAL.md."
